Hello hacker.

We've been watching you.
You think you're ready, but you aren't.  Not until you pass our tests.

Now that you're in the system, you can use the terminal to control it.  You
can work with files, compile and run programs, etc.  Anything you can do
with a mouse and a keyboard you can do here.  Usually more efficiently.  But
you need to be able to navigate it first.  Here are some basic commands:

ls - List segments.  Shows you the files and directories in the directory
     you are in.
cd - Change directory.  When you login you are at /home/user.  If you enter
     'cd examples', you would then be in /home/user/examples.  To go back up
     a directory you'd type 'cd ..'.
pwd - Present working directory. Prints out the path of the directory you
     are currently in.
mkdir - Makes a new directory.  If you are at /home/user and type 'mkdir notes',
     you will now have a directory '/home/user/notes'.
cp - Copy a file.  If the filename is 'blah', you could write a command like
     'cp blah new_blah' to make a copy of it.
mv - Move a file.  If you were in your /home/user directory and there is a file
     called 'blah', you could move it to your notes directory you made with
     'mv blah notes/.'.  The '.' on the end of that command means you still
     want to call it 'blah'.  If you didn't, you could give it a new name with a
     command like 'mv blah notes/new_blah'.
rm - Remove a file or directory.  Typing 'rm notes' in a directory that holds a
     file called 'blah' will remove it. To remove an entire directory you'd need
     to add some extra to the command; 'rm -rf notes' would delete the notes
     directory if you were in the directory that holds it.  The -rf means
     delete all the files in the directory first, then the directory.
cat - Concatenates files.  Mostly we will use it to show a file's contents.  For
     instance, 'cat /home/user/notes/note_one' would print the text of a file
     called 'note_one' in the /home/user/notes directory (if there is one there).
echo - Print a message.  If you redirect the output to a file you can create a file
     with the text.  For instance, "echo 'Hello world.' > my_file'.

These are the basic commands you will need to begin your training. They need to
be muscle memory for you.  Use them to figure out how to progress.  You'll pick up
other commands as you go.  Start in the level1 directory and search for hints of
what to do next.

